{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q003_actin_like_atpase_divergence","answer":"In SCOPe 2.08 the three catalytic domains are in one superfamily, c.55.1 "Actin-like ATPase domain" (sunid 53067). The superfamily is in fold c.55 "Ribonuclease H-like motif" (sunid 53066) and class c, "Alpha and beta proteins (a/b)". Each sits in its own family: Actin/HSP70 is c.55.1.1 (sunid 53068), Hexokinase is c.55.1.3 (sunid 53083, which I traced upward through its parents), and Glycerol kinase is c.55.1.4 (sunid 53089). I confirmed all three as children of c.55.1. They are not in the same family. Hexokinase also has a separate Glucokinase family, c.55.1.7, in the same superfamily.

What this permits: SCOPe's superfamily level asserts probable common ancestry despite low sequence identity. So you can infer that the three are divergent homologs of one ancestral actin-like ATPase/phosphotransferase core. You can also infer that their shared structural core (the two-lobed a/b ATP-binding architecture) is evolutionarily conserved. The different family placements say they are more distant than within-family relatives. Their cellular roles (cytoskeletal polymer, chaperone, sugar kinase, glycerol kinase) arose by functional divergence from that common scaffold.

What it does not permit: it does not establish identical or transferable function, substrate, reaction, catalytic mechanism or regulation. It does not support using one structure as an interchangeable template for another, nor does it support a close sequence-level model. It does not give residue-level alignments or an evolutionary history. The shared fold (c.55) alone would only mean structural similarity. It is the superfamily placement that carries the homology claim. I did not retrieve domain-level annotations or homology caveat records for specific member domains, so no per-domain artifact or heterogeneity caveats are checked here.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","mcp__scope__search_scop","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53084/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.55.1/children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=hexokinase"]}]}
